add('Task 4.2 · PM shares the full global comparison',table=[['Variable','Compared settings'],['Frequency plan','Uniform and selected sixteen-block schedules'],['Atmosphere','Standard and January sounding'],['Elevation','30°, 45°, 60°, 90°'],['Reference + sample time','2 s, 20 s, 100 s'],['Assumed calibration residual','0, 0.0001, 0.001 dB'],['Receiver noise figure','6 dB and 17 dB']],widths=[490,646],result='288 conditions × 8 outputs = 2,304 rows. Each output has 270 computed and 18 rejected conditions.',limit='No valid 95% response limit for PM2.5 or PM10 anywhere in this grid.',notes='Fixed 23 dBm transmit power, matched reference weather, 10 GHz residual correlation. All rejected cases are standard atmosphere, 30° elevation, 17 dB noise figure for both schedules and all times/calibrations. Their target separation fails the numerical gate. Failed rows remain present and do not receive fabricated zero recall.',sources=['results/joint_receiver_revision/global_comparison.csv','results/joint_receiver_revision/global_comparison_protocol.json'])
add('Task 4.3 · Detection limits and percentage definitions',body=['Fixed a 1% family false-alarm budget across eight outputs before observing outcomes.','Solved the concentration for nominal 95% power using positive-signal variance, then tested it with fresh simulated responses.'],formula='z = Φ⁻¹(1 − 0.01/8)       detect if ĉj > z snull\nc95 = z snull + 1.64485 spositive(c95)',explain='Positive absorption changes SNR and variance. The solution stays within at most 1 dB added absorption.',result='Reports include low recalls, missed detections, exact binomial intervals and concentration RMSE.',limit='95% detection power, a 95% confidence interval and 5% concentration error describe different quantities.',notes='Recall = TP/(TP+FN). False alarms = FP/(FP+TN). Relative RMSE = sqrt(mean((estimate−truth)^2))/truth. Precision and F1 use artificial 50% prevalence. A missing in-domain solution remains unavailable. The PM10 output participates in the family although it derives from two masses.',sources=[GUIDE,'scripts/evaluate_joint_receiver.py'])
